      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Einstein’s photoelectric equation gives the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons as K_max = h·ν – φ.",
      "evidence": "Energy conservation for a photon of energy hν liberating an electron with work function φ; experimentally verified linear dependence of kinetic energy on frequency.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The stopping (retarding) potential V_s satisfies e·V_s = K_max.",
      "evidence": "By definition, the stopping potential is the voltage that just reduces the photocurrent to zero, i.e., it must remove the kinetic energy of the most energetic electrons.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Therefore V_s = (h/e)·ν – φ/e, a linear function of the incident frequency ν.",
      "evidence": "Algebraic substitution of K_max from step 1 into the relation of step 2 and solving for V_s.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The linear relation holds only for frequencies above the threshold ν₀ = φ/h; below ν₀ no electrons are emitted.",
      "evidence": "Photon energy must exceed the work function for emission; this is the experimentally observed cutoff frequency.",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Option D states exactly this: the stopping potential is a linear function of frequency above the threshold frequency.",
      "evidence": "Option D wording matches the conclusion of steps 3 and 4.",
